  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nUpper ridging will continue to amplify over the Plains tonight\nand into the weekend. Several weak shortwaves riding through the\nupper ridge will bring on and off shower and thunderstorm\nchances through the next few days. There is some probability of\nfog redeveloping in our far eastern counties as well as running\nup into the higher terrain on the edge of the northern Red \nRiver Valley. On the other hand, there will be some mid level \nclouds coming in tonight with one of the embedded shortwaves, \nwhich may inhibit fog formation. Not much change in the pattern \nSaturday and Sunday with additional weak embedded shortwaves and\nsoutheasterly surface winds ahead of the surface trough. Flow \nbecome more southwesterly on Monday as the ridge axis shifts \neast, with another shortwave coming through. Upper troughing \nbegins to move into south central Canada on Tuesday and then off\nto the east after, leaving the area in near zonal to slightly \nnorthwesterly flow. A few weak embedded shortwaves could bring \nsome precipitation chances and above average temperatures \ncontinue. \n\n...Severe chances over Labor Day weekend...\n\nFirst of several shortwaves under the upper ridge comes in\ntonight, and with warm air advection, some showers and\nthunderstorms are possible. Main activity will be in our far\nwestern counties, although it is uncertain how far east the\ndecent elevated instability will move. Some of the CAMs have\nsome fairly healthy thunderstorms developing over southeastern\nND after 10Z tonight, while other runs have showers or nothing\nat all. The HREF has some low probabilities (10 percent) of \nstrong updraft helicity tracks early tomorrow morning, so can't \ncompletely rule out some elevated hailers but not super \nwidespread. Another shortwave coming through Saturday afternoon\nor night could bring additional convective activity, but the\ndeep layer shear is pretty unimpressive at around 25 kts.\nSlightly better shear and instability on Sunday over eastern \nND, although much will depend on how previous convection plays \nout and exact timing of the embedded waves. Best shear will be\nMonday afternoon, although CAPE values are all over the place.\nIn short, there will be several chances for a few isolated\nsevere cells to develop Saturday through Monday, but exactly\nwhere and when are uncertain.",
